    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Glycosylation is one of the most common types of post-translational protein\r\nmodifications and plays a role in myriad crucial processes from a molecular to an\r\norganismal scale. While most tissue types have relatively diverse glycomes, the\r\nbrain’s is unique both in its composition, which is dominated by high-mannose\r\nglycans—and how it changes in response to aging and cellular stress, two closely\r\nrelated processes. We interrogate the impact of both on the brain’s glycome,\r\nforming a clearer picture of changes to the mouse brain’s glycome during age\r\nwhile simultaneously developing novel tools to monitor and modulate changes the\r\nglycome undergoes more precisely. We demonstrate the profound effect of stress\r\non cell surface glycosylation as well as glycosylation patterns within the cell,\r\nespecially in the high-mannose glycan Man7 and the paucimannosidic glycan\r\nMan3-fucose. Not only does the glycoproteome change, but so too does proteome\r\nstability broadly, with implications from our inquiry that the two may be related\r\nin a manner that suggests a substantial and uncharacterized role of the\r\nglycoproteome in cellular stress and agingin the brain.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Tilton-Low, G. (2024). The Interplay between Age, Stress, and the N-Glycome. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/rb099ff6310. https://doi.org/10.25740/rb099ff6310.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
